riables should be clear by now.\n9.4 REGRESSION WITH AMIXTURE OF QUANTITATIVE AND\nQUALITATIVE REGRESSORS: THE ANCOVAMODELS\nANOVA models of the type discussed in the preceding two sections, al-\nthough common in fields such as sociology, psychology, education, and\nmarket research, are not that common in economics. Typically, in most eco-\nnomic research a regression model contains some explanatory variables\nthat are quantitative and some that are qualitative. Regression models con-\ntaining an admixture of quantitative and qualitative variables are called\nanalysis of covariance (ANCOVA) models. ANCOVA models are an exten-\nsion of the ANOVA models in that they provide a method of statistically con-\ntrolling the effects of quantitative regressors, called covariates or control\n8The data are obtained from the data disk in Arthur S. Goldberger, Introductory Economet-\nrics,Harvard University Press, Cambridge, Mass., 1998. We have already considered these data\nin Chap. 2.\n\nGujarati: Basic I. Single−Equation 9. Dummy Variable © The McGraw−Hill\nEconometrics, Fourth Regression Models Regression Models Companies, 2004\nEdition\nCHAPTER NINE: DUMMYVARIABLE REGRESSION MODELS 305\nvariables, in a model that includes both quantitative and qualitative, or\ndummy, regressors. We now illustrate the ANCOVA models.\nTo motivate the analysis, let us reconsider Example 9.1 by maintaining\nthat the average salary of public school teachers may not be different in the\nthree regions if we take into account any variables that cannot be stan-\ndardized across the regions. Consider, for example, the variable expendi-\ntureon public schools by local authorities,as public education is primarily a\nlocal and state question. To see if this is the case, we develop the following\nmodel:\nY =β +β D +β D +β X +u (9.4.1)\ni 1 2 2i 3 3i 4 i i\nwhere Y = average annual salary of public school teachers in state ($)\ni\nX = spending on public school per pupil ($)\ni\nD = 1, if the state is in t